{{also|third-degree}}
==English==

===Etymology===
The phrase comes from the practice of interrogation under torture, where three degrees of torture were recognised, of increasing intensity. In other contexts, three degrees of interrogation were recognised, with torture being the third degree.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-third degree.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{head|en|noun}}

# {{lb|en|informal}} [[intensive|Intensive]] [[rough]] [[interrogation]] in order to [[extract]] [[information]] or a [[confession]]
#: ''When I came home at 4 a.m., my dad really gave me the '''third degree'''.''

====Usage notes====
Chiefly used in the expression ''give someone the third degree''.

